          context: 'A population of insects on an island has two colour morphs, green and brown. Over a 20-year study period, during which the island’s habitat changed from open grassland to dense forest, the percentage of brown insects in the population increased from 20% to 75%.',
          prompt: 'Interpret the data described, and explain the likely cause of this trend in terms of natural selection.',
          answer:
            'The percentage of brown insects increased steadily from 20% to 75% over the 20-year period, a clear and substantial upward trend that coincides with the habitat changing from grassland to forest.',
          explanation:
            'This trend is best explained by natural selection acting through predation-based camouflage: in a forest habitat (with brown tree bark and shaded, brown-toned surroundings), brown insects would be better camouflaged from predators than green insects (which would have been better camouflaged in the earlier grassland habitat), giving brown insects a survival advantage as the habitat changed. Since colour is a heritable trait, the surviving, better-camouflaged brown insects passed the brown-colour allele on to more offspring, increasing its frequency in the population generation after generation as the forest developed.',
