          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain how the kidneys and ADH work together to regulate water balance when a person is well-hydrated (has consumed excess water).',
          answer:
            'When a person is well-hydrated, their blood becomes more dilute (lower solute concentration). Osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus detect this and reduce the release of ADH from the pituitary gland. Lower ADH levels decrease the permeability of the collecting ducts (and distal convoluted tubules) of the nephrons to water, so less water is reabsorbed back into the blood, and a larger volume of more dilute urine is produced, removing the excess water from the body and returning blood water content to normal.',
          explanation: 'This is the opposite response to what occurs during dehydration, but relies on the same underlying negative feedback mechanism: a deviation from the normal blood water concentration (in this case, too dilute) triggers a hormonal response (reduced ADH) that acts to correct the deviation, restoring normal blood concentration.',
