          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain the adaptive significance of positive geotropism shown by plant roots (growing toward gravity/downward), in contrast to the negative geotropism shown by shoots.',
          answer:
            'Roots typically grow toward gravity/downward (positive geotropism), which reliably directs them into the soil, where they can anchor the plant and access water and mineral nutrients. Shoots, in contrast, typically grow away from gravity/upward (negative geotropism), which reliably directs them above the soil surface, where their leaves can access light for photosynthesis. Together, these opposite responses to the same stimulus (gravity) ensure that each organ of the plant reliably grows in the direction most useful for its particular function, regardless of the orientation in which a seed happens to germinate.',
          explanation: 'This contrast again shows that the "correct" direction of a tropic response depends on the function of the organ involved, even though the same hormone (auxin) and broadly similar underlying mechanism (unequal growth due to unequal auxin distribution, with roots and shoots differing in their sensitivity to auxin) is responsible for both responses.',
