          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain why a person cannot focus clearly on both a very near object and a very distant object at exactly the same time.',
          answer:
            'Focusing sharply on an object at a particular distance requires the lens to take on one specific shape (thick and rounded for near objects, thin and flat for distant objects), achieved by a particular degree of contraction or relaxation of the ciliary muscles. Since the lens can only have one shape at any given instant, it can only bring light from objects at one particular distance (or a narrow range around it) into sharp focus on the retina at a time — light from objects at a very different distance will be focused either in front of or behind the retina, producing a blurred image for that object.',
