      items: [
        {
          id: 'lifesci-p1-2021-4-1',
          label: '4.1',
          topicId: 'life-sci-response-plants',
          grade: 12,
          difficulty: 'Easy',
          marks: 2,
          prompt: 'Define auxin.',
          answer: 'Auxin is a plant hormone, produced mainly at the tips of shoots and roots, that regulates plant growth, including the tropic (directional growth) responses to light, gravity and touch.',
          explanation: 'Auxin generally stimulates cell elongation in shoot cells at lower concentrations, but inhibits elongation in root cells at the same concentration, which is why roots and shoots can respond in opposite directions to the same auxin distribution.',
        },
        {
          id: 'lifesci-p1-2021-4-2',
          label: '4.2',
          topicId: 'life-sci-response-plants',
          grade: 12,
          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain, in terms of auxin, why removing the apical bud (tip) of a plant stimulates the growth of lateral (side) buds lower down the stem (a phenomenon called apical dominance being removed).',
          answer:
            'The apical bud at the tip of a shoot produces a high concentration of auxin, which travels down the stem and suppresses (inhibits) the growth of lateral buds further down, a phenomenon called apical dominance. When the apical bud is removed, this main source of inhibitory auxin is eliminated, so the lateral buds are no longer suppressed and are free to begin growing, producing a bushier plant with multiple side branches.',
          explanation: 'This is the physiological basis for the common gardening practice of "pinching out" or pruning the growing tip of a plant, which removes apical dominance and encourages a fuller, more branched growth habit.',
        },
        {
          id: 'lifesci-p1-2021-4-3',
          label: '4.3',
          topicId: 'life-sci-response-plants',
          grade: 12,
          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain how synthetic auxins are used as selective herbicides to kill broadleaf weeds in a field of grass (such as a cereal crop or lawn), without harming the grass itself.',
          answer:
            'Synthetic auxins applied at a relatively high concentration cause broadleaf plants (dicotyledons, such as most weeds) to grow in a rapid, uncontrolled and disorganised way — far faster than the plant’s vascular and structural tissues can support — which disrupts normal growth and eventually kills the plant. Grasses (monocotyledons, such as cereal crops and lawn grass) are far less sensitive to this synthetic auxin at the same concentration and are able to tolerate and break down the herbicide without suffering this same disruptive overgrowth, allowing the grass to survive largely unharmed while the broadleaf weeds around it are killed.',
          explanation: 'This selective toxicity — the same chemical badly overstimulating one group of plants (broadleaf weeds) while barely affecting another group (grasses) at the same dose — is the basis of widely used agricultural and lawn-care herbicides, allowing weeds to be controlled without needing to physically remove them from among the desired crop or grass.',
        },
      ],
