          explanation: 'A diverging lens compensates for the eye’s excessive natural refractive power (or its too-long eyeball) by reducing how strongly the light is bent before it even reaches the eye, effectively "correcting" the eye’s own excessive focusing power so the image lands correctly on the retina.',
