          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain how a simple laboratory experiment could be designed to demonstrate that auxin (rather than some other factor) is responsible for the phototropic bending of a seedling shoot toward light.',
          answer:
            'A control group of seedling shoots is exposed to light from one side and simply allowed to bend as normal, confirming the expected phototropic response. In an experimental group, the very tip of the shoot (where auxin is produced) is removed, or covered with an opaque cap, before exposing the seedlings to light from one side in the same way; if auxin (produced at the tip) is indeed responsible for the bending response, these decapitated or capped seedlings should fail to bend toward the light, since the source of auxin has been removed or its ability to respond to the directional light stimulus has been blocked, unlike the intact control seedlings, which continue to bend normally.',
          explanation: 'This type of comparative experiment (removing or blocking the shoot tip and comparing the result to an untreated control) isolates the specific role of the shoot tip (and the auxin produced there) in causing the phototropic response, distinguishing it from other possible explanations for the bending.',
