          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain why a mutation that changes one codon into a premature stop codon (a "nonsense" mutation) generally has a much more severe effect on a protein than a silent or a typical substitution mutation.',
          answer:
            'A premature stop codon causes translation to end immediately at that point, rather than continuing to the normal end of the gene. This means the resulting protein is significantly shortened (truncated), missing its entire remaining sequence of amino acids from that point onward. Because a protein’s three-dimensional shape (and hence its function) depends on its complete amino acid sequence, this truncation typically destroys the protein’s normal structure and function almost entirely, unlike a silent mutation (no change to the amino acid sequence at all) or a typical substitution (which changes only a single amino acid within an otherwise complete, normal-length protein).',
          explanation: 'This is why nonsense mutations (producing a premature stop codon) are generally considered among the most severe types of point mutation, since losing a large portion of a protein’s sequence is far more disruptive than a change affecting only a single amino acid.',
