          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain why crossing over during meiosis is considered essential for producing genetic variation, even though independent assortment alone already produces an enormous number of possible gamete combinations.',
          answer:
            'Independent assortment alone can only shuffle whole, intact chromosomes into different combinations — it cannot change the specific combination of alleles present along any single chromosome, since each chromosome is inherited as one unbroken unit under independent assortment alone. Crossing over, by exchanging segments of DNA between non-sister chromatids of homologous chromosomes, creates entirely new combinations of alleles within individual chromosomes themselves, combinations that could never arise from independent assortment alone, since independent assortment only rearranges which existing (unmodified) chromosomes end up together, not the allele combinations within them.',
          explanation: 'This is why crossing over and independent assortment are described as two distinct, complementary sources of genetic variation: independent assortment shuffles whole chromosomes between gametes, while crossing over additionally reshuffles the genetic content within individual chromosomes, together producing a far greater range of possible gamete genotypes than either mechanism could alone.',
