          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Explain the importance of the thin film of moisture and surfactant lining the inner surface of each alveolus.',
          answer:
            'The thin film of moisture lining each alveolus allows respiratory gases (oxygen and carbon dioxide) to dissolve before diffusing across the alveolar and capillary walls, since gases diffuse far more effectively in solution than directly through a dry membrane. A substance called surfactant is also present in this fluid, reducing its surface tension; without surfactant, the surface tension of the moisture film would tend to make the moist alveolar walls stick together and collapse, especially in the smallest alveoli.',
          explanation: 'This is why premature babies, who may not yet produce sufficient surfactant, can suffer from respiratory distress, since their alveoli are prone to collapsing — a real medical illustration of just how important this feature is.',
