          difficulty: 'Challenge',
          marks: 6,
          context: 'At high altitude, the atmospheric partial pressure of oxygen is significantly lower than at sea level, even though oxygen still makes up 21% of the air.',
          prompt: 'Explain why a person moving from sea level to a high-altitude environment initially experiences shortness of breath, and describe ONE way the body can adapt to this over several weeks.',
          answer:
            'At high altitude, air pressure is lower, so although oxygen still makes up 21% of the air, its actual partial pressure (concentration) available to diffuse into the blood at the alveoli is much lower than at sea level, reducing the concentration gradient driving oxygen diffusion from air into blood. Less oxygen therefore diffuses into the blood per breath, causing the person to breathe faster and more deeply — and feel short of breath — as the body attempts to take in enough oxygen. Over several weeks, the body can adapt by producing more red blood cells, increasing the blood’s haemoglobin concentration and hence its overall oxygen-carrying capacity, compensating for the reduced oxygen gradient at the alveoli.',
          explanation: 'This shows that the rate of diffusion depends on the size of a concentration (partial pressure) gradient, not merely percentage composition, and illustrates a genuine long-term physiological adaptation (acclimatisation) to chronic low oxygen availability, rather than just a temporary behavioural response.',
