          answer: 'The diaphragm relaxes and returns to its dome shape, while the external intercostal muscles relax, allowing the rib cage to move downward and inward under gravity and elastic recoil. Together, these movements decrease the volume of the thoracic cavity, which increases the air pressure inside the lungs relative to the pressure outside the body; because air flows from higher to lower pressure, air is forced out of the lungs through the airways.',
