          answer: 'Bilateral symmetry is a body plan in which an organism’s body can be divided into two mirror-image halves along a single plane running from its head to its tail end.',
          explanation: 'Bilateral symmetry is closely associated with an animal having a distinct head end (cephalisation), where sensory organs and a concentration of nerve tissue are typically found, well suited to an animal that actively moves in a consistent forward direction.',
