          topicId: 'life-sci-population-ecology',
          grade: 11,
          difficulty: 'Moderate',
          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Distinguish between immigration and emigration, and explain how each affects the size of a local population, independently of births and deaths.',
          answer:
            'Immigration is the movement of individuals into a population from elsewhere, while emigration is the movement of individuals out of a population to elsewhere. Immigration increases the size of the local population, adding individuals that were not born within it, while emigration decreases the size of the local population by removing individuals from it, even though those individuals are not dead — they have simply relocated elsewhere. Together with birth rate and death rate, immigration and emigration make up all four factors that together determine how a local population’s size changes over time.',
          explanation: 'Because immigration and emigration can significantly affect local population size even when the birth rate and death rate remain unchanged, a population’s size cannot be fully understood or predicted from its birth and death rates alone, particularly in habitats that are not fully isolated from surrounding areas.',
