          prompt: 'Describe what happens during the lag phase and the exponential (log) phase of population growth, and explain why growth is initially slow before becoming rapid.',
          answer: 'During the lag phase, only a small number of individuals are present, so even though each is reproducing, the total number of new individuals added to the population remains low; the population is also often still adjusting to its new environment. During the exponential (log) phase, the population has grown large enough that a much greater number of individuals are now reproducing simultaneously, and resources remain abundant relative to this still-growing population, so the population increases very rapidly.',
