          answer:
            'A virus attaches to the surface of a compatible host cell and injects (or otherwise introduces) its genetic material into that cell. Once inside, the viral genetic material hijacks the host cell’s own cellular machinery, instructing it to produce many copies of the viral genetic material and viral protein coats instead of carrying out its normal functions. These new components then assemble into many new virus particles within the host cell, which are eventually released — often by causing the host cell to burst (lyse) — to go on and infect further host cells.',
