          answer: 'The lag phase is the initial period of population growth, during which population size increases only slowly. This slow growth typically occurs because the population starts from a very small number of individuals, so even a high reproduction rate per individual results in only a small total number of new individuals added, and because the population may still be adjusting physiologically or behaviourally to a new environment.',
