          answer:
            'During the exponential (log) phase, resources such as food, water and space remain abundant relative to the size of the still-growing population, so there is little competition restricting reproduction, and the population grows very rapidly. During the stationary phase, the population has grown large enough that it approaches the environment’s carrying capacity, so resources become increasingly limited relative to the number of individuals present, intensifying competition and slowing population growth until it levels off.',
          explanation: 'The shift from the exponential to the stationary phase therefore reflects a change in resource availability relative to population size, not a change in the resources themselves — the same environment can support rapid growth early on and comparatively little further growth later, simply because a larger population is now sharing the same finite resource base.',
