          marks: 6,
          prompt: 'Explain what is meant by a density-dependent limiting factor, giving TWO examples, other than food supply, disease, predation and nesting site competition, of such factors that can limit population growth.',
          answer:
            'A density-dependent limiting factor is a factor whose effect on population growth intensifies as population density increases. Examples: (1) Competition for territory or mates — as population density rises, more individuals compete for a limited number of suitable territories or mates, reducing the reproductive success of a growing proportion of the population. (2) Stress-related effects of overcrowding — in some species, very high population density can itself trigger physiological stress responses, such as reduced fertility or increased aggression, that lower reproduction or survival, effects that become more pronounced the more crowded the population becomes.',
