          marks: 5,
          prompt: 'Describe how carbon dioxide is incorporated into organic molecules during the light-independent reactions (Calvin cycle), and state the ultimate product of this cycle.',
          answer:
            'Carbon dioxide diffusing into the stroma is fixed onto an existing 5-carbon acceptor molecule, in a reaction catalysed by an enzyme, forming an unstable 6-carbon compound that immediately splits into two 3-carbon molecules. Using the ATP and NADPH supplied by the light-dependent reactions, these 3-carbon molecules are converted through several steps into glucose, while some are also used to regenerate the original 5-carbon acceptor so the cycle can continue.',
