          answer: 'As photosynthesis proceeds inside a sealed container, the plant steadily uses up the available carbon dioxide, causing its concentration inside the container to fall. Once carbon dioxide concentration drops low enough, it becomes the limiting factor, restricting the rate of the light-independent reactions (and therefore the overall rate of photosynthesis) even though light intensity itself has not changed.',
          explanation: 'This scenario is a practical illustration of how a factor that was not initially limiting can become limiting purely as a consequence of the reaction itself proceeding — here, photosynthesis consumes its own required raw material until that raw material becomes scarce enough to restrict the process.',
