          prompt: 'A person has had part of their small intestine surgically removed due to disease. Explain why this is likely to reduce their ability to absorb sufficient nutrients from their diet.',
          answer: 'Removing part of the small intestine reduces the total length (and therefore the total number of villi and microvilli) available for absorption, substantially decreasing the overall surface area across which digested nutrients can be absorbed into the blood. With less absorptive surface area available, food may pass through the remaining intestine too quickly for all of the nutrients it contains to be fully absorbed, resulting in some nutrients being lost in the faeces instead.',
