          prompt: 'Compare the amount of ATP released, and the extent to which the glucose molecule is broken down, in aerobic respiration versus anaerobic respiration.',
          answer:
            'Aerobic respiration fully breaks glucose down all the way to carbon dioxide and water, releasing a large amount of ATP per glucose molecule. Anaerobic respiration only partially breaks glucose down (for example, into lactic acid, or into ethanol and carbon dioxide), releasing considerably less ATP per glucose molecule, since most of the chemical energy originally present in the glucose remains locked within the incompletely broken-down end product.',
          explanation: 'This is why an organism relying solely on anaerobic respiration must break down far more glucose than an aerobically respiring organism to obtain the same total amount of usable ATP.',
