          prompt: 'Explain why a collapsed lung (in which air enters the pleural space between the lung and the chest wall) severely impairs a person’s ability to breathe using that lung.',
          answer: 'Normally, the lung is held against the inside of the chest wall by the thin layer of fluid within the sealed pleural space, allowing it to expand and contract in step with the movements of the rib cage and diaphragm. If air enters this pleural space (for example, through a puncture wound), it breaks the seal that holds the lung against the chest wall, so the lung is no longer pulled outward when the thoracic cavity expands, and it can partially or completely collapse, sharply reducing the volume of air that lung can take in during breathing.',
