          answer: 'Fungi obtain nutrition heterotrophically, by secreting digestive enzymes onto external organic matter and then absorbing the resulting digested nutrients; they cannot photosynthesise, since they lack chlorophyll. Although both fungi and plants are non-motile and often appear to grow from a fixed location, fungi are classified separately because they lack chlorophyll and photosynthetic ability, have cell walls made of chitin rather than cellulose, and obtain nutrition externally through absorption rather than internally through photosynthesis.',
