          answer: 'A snail (mollusc) moves using a single, broad, muscular foot, which produces slow, wave-like muscular contractions against a surface (often aided by a trail of secreted mucus) to achieve slow crawling movement. A fish (chordate) moves by contracting muscles arranged in blocks (myotomes) along either side of its body and internal skeleton (backbone), producing side-to-side undulating movements of the body and tail that push against the surrounding water to achieve swimming.',
